27. PRIVACY, SECURITY, AND LOCAL STORAGE

Privacy is a core architectural requirement.

27.1 Storage rules

Use IndexedDB for:

- Transactions.
- Accounts.
- People and merchants.
- Categories and tags.
- Statement metadata.
- Import mappings.
- Classification rules.
- Budgets and goals.
- Recurring commitments.
- Linked transaction relationships.
- Audit history.
- Other persistent application records.

Use LocalStorage only for small preferences such as the theme or selected tab.

Do not store large statements or financial datasets in LocalStorage.

Store uploaded files only when necessary and give users explicit file-management controls.

27.2 No remote financial data transmission

Do not implement:

- A backend database.
- Cloud synchronization.
- Automatic statement uploads.
- Remote financial analytics.
- Automatic remote OCR.
- Automatic remote AI processing.
- Third-party financial tracking or telemetry.
- External bank connections.

Do not include analytics scripts that can expose financial activity.

External dependencies may be downloaded as application code, but transaction records and uploaded documents must not be transmitted to dependency providers.

Use a strict and documented network policy. If practical, provide a mode that blocks all unnecessary external network requests after the application has loaded.

Clearly document any unavoidable external requests.

27.3 Local encryption

Explain that browser-local storage is not automatically encrypted.

If implementing encrypted data storage, use a vetted cryptographic design based on browser-supported cryptography, secure key derivation, and authenticated encryption.

Do not invent cryptographic algorithms.

Do not store encryption passwords in plain text.

Make encryption and recovery behavior explicit. Avoid locking users out of their only copy of their data.

If robust encryption cannot be completed, state that limitation and recommend protecting the device and browser profile.

Visual masking, PIN gates, and hidden transactions must not be described as equivalent to encryption.

27.4 Privacy settings

Provide controls for:

- Viewing storage usage where supported.
- Exporting all data.
- Creating full backups.
- Restoring backups.
- Exporting selected records.
- Deleting individual statements.
- Deleting associated transactions.
- Clearing application data.
- Managing privacy settings.
- Understanding local storage limitations.

Require explicit confirmation for irreversible actions.

27.5 Data migration

Use versioned database schemas and safe migration logic.

Never overwrite the user's existing database with an empty database during an application update.

Preserve all compatible data when adding or changing fields.

If a migration fails, show a useful error and avoid destroying the previous data.

